Got it reproduced. Patch (inlined at the end) added `abort()` at 1000 consecutive iterations where `i` doesn't advance, plus `log_debug` of the offending cell's contents and the state-machine snapshot. Ran under `-vv`, tripped after ~28 hours of agent-deck load.

## The answer: orphan `GRID_FLAG_PADDING`, not a variation selector / combining mark

STALL lines from the abort:

```
tty_draw_line: STALL i=120 px=0 py=0 nx=147 ex=147 atx=0 aty=55 wrapped=0
tty_draw_line: STALL cell: size=0 width=0 bytes=[] flags=0x4 attr=0x0 fg=8 bg=8 us=8 link=0
tty_draw_line: STALL state: current=PAD next=PAD empty=0 last.bg=8 defaults->bg=8 len=0 width=0 last_i=120
```

`flags=0x4` is `GRID_FLAG_PADDING`. `size=0`, `width=0`, `bytes=[]` — there is no Unicode data at all; this is a pure padding cell with no parent wide char alongside it.

## How the grid reached this state

The stall is in the **status line** redraw (`screen_redraw_draw_status`, `aty=55`). The line before the stall shows the same row being redrawn three times within ~440 ms. The relevant cell positions from the first (successful) call and the third (stalling) call:

**Call 1** (succeeds, `t = 0.000`):
```
cell 119 empty 0, bg 35265318; state: current SAME, next SAME    ← width-2 cell
cell 121 empty 1, bg 35265318; state: current SAME, next EMPTY   ← 120 skipped (padding of 119)
cell 122 ...
```
Position 119 has a wide character; position 120 is its `GRID_FLAG_PADDING` companion and is correctly skipped by the `i += gcp->data.width` advance.

**Call 3** (stalls, `t = 0.438`):
```
cell 117 empty 0, bg 35265318; state: current SAME, next SAME
cell 118 empty 1, bg 35265318; state: current SAME, next EMPTY
cell 119 empty 0, bg 35265318; state: current EMPTY, next SAME   ← now width-1
cell 120 empty 0, bg 8;        state: current SAME, next PAD     ← orphan PADDING cell
cell 120 empty 0, bg 8;        state: current PAD, next PAD      ← repeats forever
```

Between the two calls, agent-deck issued `set-option -g status-left <new string>`. It does this on a 2-second ticker plus on every session state transition ([`internal/ui/home.go:2679`](https://github.com/asheshgoplani/agent-deck/blob/95608cb/internal/ui/home.go#L2679), [`:2696`](https://github.com/asheshgoplani/agent-deck/blob/95608cb/internal/ui/home.go#L2696), [`internal/tmux/tmux.go:4404`](https://github.com/asheshgoplani/agent-deck/blob/95608cb/internal/tmux/tmux.go#L4404)). The format template starts with `⚡` (U+26A1, width 2) and includes per-session status glyphs drawn from `● ◐ ○ ✕ ■` ([`internal/session/notifications.go:234-248`](https://github.com/asheshgoplani/agent-deck/blob/95608cb/internal/session/notifications.go#L234-L248)) whose width is terminal-dependent (East Asian Ambiguous). When a session's state changes, the glyph at the session's slot in the string can flip between a width-2 and width-1 character — which is consistent with cell 119 going from width-2 to width-1 between call 1 and call 3.

Tmux wrote the narrow character at 119 but did not clear `GRID_FLAG_PADDING` on cell 120. Cell 120 is now a padding cell with no wide-char parent — `flags=0x4`, `size=0`, `width=0`, `bg=8`.

## Why the loop can't escape cell 120

Walking through `tty_draw_line` with that cell:

1. `px >= ex` is false, so `empty` is computed from the `bg != last.bg` check or `tty_draw_line_get_empty`.
2. In call 3, `last.bg=35265318` (from cell 119's truecolor status-left bg) but `gcp->bg=8` (default bg on the orphan), so `empty = 0` via the `bg != last.bg` branch.
3. `next_state = TTY_DRAW_LINE_PAD` ([`tty-draw.c:268`](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/blob/participant4/tty-draw.c#L268): `else if (gcp->flags & GRID_FLAG_PADDING)`).
4. First iteration: state change SAME→PAD triggers a flush, then `last_i = i = 120`, `current_state = PAD`, `last = gcp` (so `last.bg = 8`).
5. Second iteration: cell 120 again. Now `gcp->bg == last.bg == 8`, so `empty = tty_draw_line_get_empty(gcp, nx - i)`. That function returns 0 because `data.width == 0` fails the `!=1 && >nx` check, and the cell has no CLEARED/TAB flag and no space byte. So `empty = 0`, `next_state = PAD`.
6. `next_state == current_state`, no flush. Not EMPTY or PAD append skip. Not DONE. `advance = (empty != 0) ? empty : gcp->data.width = 0 || 0 = 0`. **`i` doesn't change.** Livelock.

## Fix

The fix presumably belongs somewhere in the wide-char-overwrite handling — I didn't trace it end to end.

Build: [`HEAD-participant4`](https://github.com/tmux/tmux/commit/participant4) + the patch below, macOS 26.4.1 arm64, agent-deck v1.7.67 workload (status-left updated globally every 2 s plus on session state transitions; livelock reproduced after ~28 h with a single active session).

<details>
<summary>Diagnostic patch (<code>livelock-diagnostic.patch</code>)</summary>

```diff
diff --git a/tty-draw.c b/tty-draw.c
index b5ecbe50..9f716760 100644
--- a/tty-draw.c
+++ b/tty-draw.c
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@

 #include <sys/types.h>

+#include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>

 #include "tmux.h"
@@ -323,6 +324,59 @@ tty_draw_line(struct tty *tty, struct screen *s, u_int px, u_int py, u_int nx,
 		if (next_state == TTY_DRAW_LINE_DONE)
 			break;

+		/* Livelock diagnostic: issue #5024. */
+		{
+			static u_int stall_prev_i = (u_int)-1;
+			static u_int stall_count = 0;
+			u_int advance = (empty != 0) ? empty : gcp->data.width;
+			if (advance == 0) {
+				if (i == stall_prev_i) {
+					stall_count++;
+				} else {
+					stall_prev_i = i;
+					stall_count = 1;
+				}
+				if (stall_count >= 1000) {
+					char hex[128];
+					size_t dn = gcp->data.size;
+					u_int n;
+					if (dn > 32) dn = 32;
+					hex[0] = '\0';
+					for (n = 0; n < dn; n++) {
+						snprintf(hex + 3*n, sizeof hex - 3*n,
+						    "%02x ",
+						    (unsigned char)gcp->data.data[n]);
+					}
+					log_debug("%s: STALL i=%u px=%u py=%u nx=%u "
+					    "ex=%u atx=%u aty=%u wrapped=%d",
+					    __func__, i, px, py, nx, ex, atx, aty,
+					    wrapped);
+					log_debug("%s: STALL cell: size=%u width=%u "
+					    "bytes=[%s] flags=0x%x attr=0x%x "
+					    "fg=%d bg=%d us=%d link=%u",
+					    __func__,
+					    (u_int)gcp->data.size,
+					    (u_int)gcp->data.width,
+					    hex, gcp->flags, gcp->attr,
+					    gcp->fg, gcp->bg, gcp->us,
+					    (u_int)gcp->link);
+					log_debug("%s: STALL state: current=%s "
+					    "next=%s empty=%d last.bg=%d "
+					    "defaults->bg=%d len=%zu width=%u "
+					    "last_i=%u",
+					    __func__,
+					    tty_draw_line_states[current_state],
+					    tty_draw_line_states[next_state],
+					    empty, last.bg, defaults->bg,
+					    len, width, last_i);
+					abort();
+				}
+			} else {
+				stall_prev_i = (u_int)-1;
+				stall_count = 0;
+			}
+		}
+
 		/* Otherwise move to the next. */
 		current_state = next_state;
 		memcpy(&last, gcp, sizeof last);
```

</details>

Good, thanks. This is useful but I can't see where the stray padding is coming from.

Here is a diff which adds some checking after padding is erased which might make it show up:  [check_pad2.diff.txt](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/27400956/check_pad2.diff.txt)

We should probably also handle this better in `tty_draw_line`. I think  we shouldn't end up with `TTY_DRAW_LINE_PAD` in the normal case because it should be skipped when we add on the width, so maybe this would be enough:
~~~~
--- tty-draw.c  4 Apr 2026 11:39:49 -0000       1.7
+++ tty-draw.c  5 May 2026 12:50:02 -0000
@@ -328,6 +328,8 @@ tty_draw_line(struct tty *tty, struct sc
                memcpy(&last, gcp, sizeof last);
                if (empty != 0)
                        i += empty;
+               else if (current_state == TTY_DRAW_LINE_PAD)
+                       i++;
                else
                        i += gcp->data.width;
        }
~~~~
Or possibly we should just treat `PAD` as `EMPTY` (what is the difference really?).

